{
  "summary": "The round-3 synthesis is a solid pragmatic framework but has several unresolved gaps. First, blocking semantics are applied uniformly across all stages, but dependencies should only gate implementation — ideation and planning don't require upstream features to be 'done', so is_blocked() needs stage-aware behavior or the auto-run skip should only apply in impl_stages, not plan_stages. Second, treating 'rejected' as a resolved state that unblocks downstream features is dangerous without plan invalidation: if feature B depends on feature A and A is rejected, B's plan was designed assuming A exists — auto-proceeding with an invalid plan will produce broken implementations. The pipeline needs to either flag B for re-planning or require human confirmation before unblocking on rejection. Third, the 'cascading cleanup on delete' assumes a delete path that doesn't exist — FeatureFile has no delete method, features live as JSON files moved between stage directories, and 'rejected' is a stage not a deletion. The cleanup trigger and mechanism need concrete definition (on move_to_stage('rejected')? on actual file removal? both?). Fourth, existing feature JSON files lack the depends_on field entirely — the implementation needs graceful defaulting (depends_on = self._data.get('depends_on', [])) to avoid KeyError on every existing feature. Fifth, the unblock-notification scan in move_to_stage() has no concurrency consideration — the pipeline server can process multiple stage transitions from different clients (TUI, WebUI, auto-mode) and move_to_stage() has no locking, so simultaneous completions could produce inconsistent unblock notifications."
}
